//? Title: Kruskal forests through actual union decisions
//? Subtitle: Stable ties, disconnected graphs and the original six-vertex minimum tree
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Connecting weighted vertices as cheaply as possible requires selecting edges
without forming cycles. Kruskal processes edges by increasing weight and joins
distinct components, producing a minimum spanning tree when the graph is
connected and a minimum forest otherwise. We use the existing canonical native
work/trace API to inspect actual root decisions, retain the original six-vertex
nine-edge graph, and test stable ties, loops, disconnected inputs and the
distinction between supplied edges and scanned edges.
)

\toc

> Why the greedy edge is safe

Maintain a forest extendable to a minimum spanning forest. Its connected
components define cuts. The next accepted edge is a lightest eligible edge
crossing some current component cut. If an optimal extension lacks it, the path
joining its endpoints contains another edge crossing that cut, whose weight is
no smaller; exchange that edge for the selected one without increasing total
weight. Induction preserves optimality. An edge whose endpoints already share a
root would make a cycle, so rejecting it preserves the forest. Apply this
argument independently to disconnected input components.

The library validates the complete graph, stably sorts finite signed weights,
and uses path halving with union by rank. Equal weights preserve original input
order; they can give multiple optimum forests, so deterministic output is not a
uniqueness proof. Self-loops always reject, parallel edges retain distinct
identities, and negative weights are valid. The scan stops as soon as n−1 edges
have been accepted; sorting and complete validation still see all input records.

> Actual roots explain an accepted edge

The tiny triangle includes a negative self-loop and a duplicate parallel edge.
The diagram summarizes the first actual accepted union using the producing root
identities. The table retains rejected scans as well; these roots are union-find
implementation identities, not minimum-vertex labels.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kruskal_union.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t =
        dm::algorithms::kruskal_trace(3,
                                      {{0, 0, -1}, {0, 1, 1}, {0, 1, 1},
                                       {1, 2, 2}, {0, 2, 3}});
    std::print("| actual scan | original edge identity | first root | second root | accepted | components " "after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &e = t.steps[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, e.input_index,
                   e.root_u, e.root_v, e.accepted, e.components_after);
    }
    const auto &e =
        *std::find_if(t.steps.begin(), t.steps.end(), [](const auto &e) {
        return e.accepted;
    });
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("roots", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual pre-merge roots\n{}, {}", e.root_u, e.root_v)).node("decision", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual input edge {}\naccepted {}", e.input_index, e.accepted)).node("after", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual components after\n{}", e.components_after)).edge("roots", "decision").edge("decision", "after").save_svg("build/kruskal_union.svg");
}
```
